    "style-research ch.1 Cross-genre engine constants"
  ],
  "notes": "A kick on all four quarters of every bar, and an open hat answering on the & of every beat — that pull between the two is the whole engine of the genre, and it never stops for a fill. Claps stack on 2 and 4, the bass pumps the offbeat eighths in the holes between the kicks, and the timing is straight: the machine is supposed to sound like a machine. What separates house from the rest of the four-on-the-floor family is the harmony — seventh and ninth piano and organ chords moving through a real four-chord loop, where the other 4x4 genres sit on a drone. ⚠ **`drums.kick.lockTo808` removed (2026-08-17, 808 gate).** This kit has no 808 — `bass808` is `null` here or in the parent it inherits — and `lockTo808` is the one parameter that says *how much of the kick the 808 follows*, so in this file it described a pairing that does not exist: `generators::drums::bass808` returns on `block.is_none()` before the value is ever read, which makes it provably dead here. Where it was NOT dead was in the artists and producers who extend this kit and author an 808 of their own: they inherited the zero, `kicks.iter().filter(|_| rng.random_bool(0.0))` kept nothing, and their sub lane was silent at every seed — `bass808::every_shipped_model_with_an_808_produces_a_playable_line` reported \"no 808 at all\" for 35 of them. Removing it restores the engine's documented default of 1.0 for exactly those children and changes nothing here. ⚠ No researched claim moved: this kit's own statement about the 808 is the `bass808: null` beside it, and that is untouched.",
